DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The iiooccttll() system call manipulates the underlying device parameters of
       special  files.  In particular, many operating characteristics of char‐
       acter special files (e.g., terminals) may be  controlled  with  iiooccttll()
       operations.  The argument _f_d must be an open file descriptor.

       The  second  argument  is a device‐dependent operation code.  The third
       argument is an untyped pointer  to  memory.   It’s  traditionally  cchhaarr
       **_a_r_g_p  (from  the days before vvooiidd ** was valid C), and will be so named
       for this discussion.

       An iiooccttll() _o_p has encoded in it whether the argument is an _i_n parameter
       or _o_u_t parameter, and the size of the argument _a_r_g_p in  bytes.   Macros
       and  defines  used  in specifying an iiooccttll() _o_p are located in the file
       _<_s_y_s_/_i_o_c_t_l_._h_>.  See NOTES.

